        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "src": "p.17",
          "topic": "Mass Nouns إسْم جَماعي — Explanation; Numbers with Fruits and Vegetables; Method A, unit word 1: حَبَّة (printed page 152)",
          "explanation": "Explanation: A mass noun, also called a non count noun, is a noun that cannot be counted, such as sugar, water, and happiness. You can't say \"two waters\" or \"three happinesses.\" In Arabic, most of the words for fruits and vegetables are mass nouns, and they are masculine. They take singular verbs and adjectives but are plural in meaning. — Numbers with Fruits and Vegetables: In the sentence \"I have to buy lettuce,\" the amount is not important. But how do you say \"I want to buy four heads of lettuce\"? There are two methods of specifying a number in such cases. Some fruits and vegetables require one method, and others require a different method. For a few fruits and vegetables, you can use either method. — Method A: To specify one or more units of a fruit or vegetable, use a \"unit word\" before the noun. Examples of English unit words are head (of lettuce), bunch (of parsley), grain (of salt), and slice (of bread). Arabic unit words are related to the shape of the fruit or vegetable. — 1. حَبَّة (grain; dual حَبَّتِين plural, حَبّات): This unit word is used with most fruits and vegetables that are round and can sit in the palm of the hand. The word is also used with vegetables that have a grainlike shape.",
          "examples": [
            {"ar": "خيار كبير", "en": "big cucumbers"},
            {"ar": "جَزَر حِلو", "en": "sweet carrots"},
            {"ar": "حَبَّة بَنْدورة", "en": "one tomato"},
            {"ar": "حَبَّتِين بَنْدُورة", "en": "two tomatoes"},
            {"ar": "عَشَر حَبّات بَزيلة", "en": "ten peas"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "src": "p.16",
          "topic": "Mass Nouns — Method A unit words 2-4 (راس, قَرْن, ظُمَّة); Method B steps 1-2 (printed page 153)",
          "explanation": "2. راس (head; dual راسِين plural روس): This unit word is used with garlic and with some vegetables that have layers. — 3. قَرْن (horn; dual قَرْنِين plural, قُرون): This unit word is used with fruits and vegetables that are shaped like a long, slightly curved antelope horn. — 4. ظُمَّة (bunch; dual ظُمَّتين plural, ظِمَم): This unit word is used with leaves that are commonly sold in bunches. — Method B: 1. To specify one unit of a fruit or vegetable, add the feminine suffix \"ة\" to the noun. 2. To specify two units, use the dual suffix een. — Handwritten pencil notes on this page: 'always pronounce Tar-nas-uta' beside ظُمَّة, and 'قَطْف قطفين قْطوف / عنب grapes' in the bottom corner.",
